              - generic:
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Creates a
                  - code: Vec<T>
                  - text: where each element is produced by calling
                  - code: f
                  - text: with that element’s index while walking forward through the
                  - code: Vec<T>
                  - text: .
                - paragraph: This is essentially the same as writing
                - code: vec![f(0), f(1), f(2), …, f(length - 2), f(length - 1)]
                - paragraph:
                  - text: and is similar to
                  - code: (0..i).map(f)
                  - text: ", just for"
                  - code: Vec<T>
                  - text: s not iterators.
                - paragraph:
                  - text: If
                  - code: length == 0
                  - text: ", this produces an empty"
                  - code: Vec<T>
                  - text: without ever calling
                  - code: f
                  - text: .
                - heading "Example" [level=5] [ref=f84e1122]
                - code: "#![feature(vec_from_fn)] let vec = Vec::from_fn(5, |i| i); // indexes are: 0 1 2 3 4 assert_eq!(vec, [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]); let vec2 = Vec::from_fn(8, |i| i * 2); // indexes are: 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 assert_eq!(vec2, [0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14]); let bool_vec = Vec::from_fn(5, |i| i % 2 == 0); // indexes are: 0 1 2 3 4 assert_eq!(bool_vec, [true, false, true, false, true]);"
                - paragraph:
                  - text: The
                  - code: Vec<T>
                  - text: "is generated in ascending index order, starting from the front and going towards the back, so you can use closures with mutable state:"
                - code: "#![feature(vec_from_fn)] let mut state = 1; let a = Vec::from_fn(6, |_| { let x = state; state *= 2; x }); assert_eq!(a, [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32]);"
