            - group:
              - generic:
                - generic:
                  - link "Source" [ref=f84e1374]: ../../src/alloc/vec/mod.rs.html#1185-1190
                  - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e1375]:
                    - text: pub const unsafe fn
                    - link "from_raw_parts_in" [ref=f84e1376]
                    - text: "( ptr:"
                    - link "*mut T" [ref=f84e1377]
                    - text: ", length:"
                    - link "usize" [ref=f84e1378]
                    - text: ", capacity:"
                    - link "usize" [ref=f84e1379]
                    - text: ", alloc: A, ) ->"
                    - link "Vec" [ref=f84e1380]
                    - text: <T, A>
                - generic:
                  - text: � 🔬
                  - generic:
                    - text: This is a nightly-only experimental API. (
                    - code: allocator_api
                    - link "#32838" [ref=f84e1385]
                    - text: )
                - text: Collapse
              - generic:
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Creates a
                  - code: Vec<T, A>
                  - text: directly from a pointer, a length, a capacity, and an allocator.
                - heading "Safety" [level=5] [ref=f84e1389]
                - paragraph: "This is highly unsafe, due to the number of invariants that aren’t checked:"
                - list:
                  - listitem:
                    - code: ptr
                    - text: must be
                    - link [ref=f84e1394]:
                      - /url: ../alloc/trait.Allocator.html#currently-allocated-memory
                      - emphasis: currently allocated
                    - text: via the given allocator
                    - code: alloc
                    - text: .
                  - listitem:
                    - code: T
                    - text: needs to have the same alignment as what
                    - code: ptr
                    - text: was allocated with. (
                    - code: T
                    - text: having a less strict alignment is not sufficient, the alignment really needs to be equal to satisfy the
                    - link [ref=f84e1401]:
                      - code: dealloc
                    - text: requirement that memory must be allocated and deallocated with the same layout.)
                  - listitem:
                    - text: The size of
                    - code: T
                    - text: times the
                    - code: capacity
                    - text: (i.e. the allocated size in bytes) needs to be the same size as the pointer was allocated with. (Because similar to alignment,
                    - link [ref=f84e1406]:
                      - code: dealloc
                    - text: must be called with the same layout
                    - code: size
                    - text: .)
                  - listitem:
                    - code: length
                    - text: needs to be less than or equal to
                    - code: capacity
                    - text: .
                  - listitem:
                    - text: The first
                    - code: length
                    - text: values must be properly initialized values of type
                    - code: T
                    - text: .
                  - listitem:
                    - code: capacity
                    - text: needs to
                    - link [ref=f84e1417]:
                      - /url: ../alloc/trait.Allocator.html#memory-fitting
                      - emphasis: fit
                    - text: the layout size that the pointer was allocated with.
                  - listitem:
                    - text: The allocated size in bytes must be no larger than
                    - code: isize::MAX
                    - text: . See the safety documentation of
                    - link [ref=f84e1421]:
                      - code: pointer::offset
                    - text: .
                - paragraph:
                  - text: These requirements are always upheld by any
                  - code: ptr
                  - text: that has been allocated via
                  - code: Vec<T, A>
                  - text: . Other allocation sources are allowed if the invariants are upheld.
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Violating these may cause problems like corrupting the allocator’s internal data structures. For example it is
                  - strong: not
                  - text: safe to build a
                  - code: Vec<u8>
                  - text: from a pointer to a C
                  - code: char
                  - text: array with length
                  - code: size_t
                  - text: . It’s also not safe to build one from a
                  - code: Vec<u16>
                  - text: and its length, because the allocator cares about the alignment, and these two types have different alignments. The buffer was allocated with alignment 2 (for
                  - code: u16
                  - text: ), but after turning it into a
                  - code: Vec<u8>
                  - text: it’ll be deallocated with alignment 1.
                - paragraph:
                  - text: The ownership of
                  - code: ptr
                  - text: is effectively transferred to the
                  - code: Vec<T>
                  - text: which may then deallocate, reallocate or change the contents of memory pointed to by the pointer at will. Ensure that nothing else uses the pointer after calling this function.
                - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e1437]
                - code: "#![feature(allocator_api)] use std::alloc::System; use std::ptr; let mut v = Vec::with_capacity_in(3, System); v.push(1); v.push(2); v.push(3); // Deconstruct the vector into parts. let (p, len, cap, alloc) = v.into_raw_parts_with_alloc(); unsafe { // Overwrite memory with 4, 5, 6 for i in 0..len { ptr::write(p.add(i), 4 + i); } // Put everything back together into a Vec let rebuilt = Vec::from_raw_parts_in(p, len, cap, alloc.clone()); assert_eq!(rebuilt, [4, 5, 6]); }"
                - paragraph: "Using memory that was allocated elsewhere:"
                - code: "#![feature(allocator_api)] use std::alloc::{AllocError, Allocator, Global, Layout}; fn main() { let layout = Layout::array::<u32>(16).expect(\"overflow cannot happen\"); let vec = unsafe { let mem = match Global.allocate(layout) { Ok(mem) => mem.cast::<u32>().as_ptr(), Err(AllocError) => return, }; mem.write(1_000_000); Vec::from_raw_parts_in(mem, 1, 16, Global) }; assert_eq!(vec, &[1_000_000]); assert_eq!(vec.capacity(), 16); }"
            - group:
              - generic:
                - generic:
                  - link "Source" [ref=f84e1448]: ../../src/alloc/vec/mod.rs.html#1300-1305
                  - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e1449]:
