                  - link [ref=f84e1960]:
                    - code: as_mut_ptr
                  - text: ", and"
                  - link [ref=f84e1962]:
                    - code: as_non_null
                  - text: . Note that calling other methods that materialize references to the slice, or references to specific elements you are planning on accessing through this pointer, may still invalidate this pointer. See the second example below for how this guarantee can be used.
                - paragraph:
                  - text: The method also guarantees that, as long as
                  - code: T
                  - text: is not zero-sized and the capacity is nonzero, the pointer may be passed into
                  - link [ref=f84e1966]:
                    - code: dealloc
                  - text: with a layout of
                  - code: Layout::array::<T>(capacity)
                  - text: in order to deallocate the backing memory. If this is done, be careful not to run the destructor of the
                  - code: Vec
                  - text: ", as dropping it will result in double-frees. Wrapping the"
                  - code: Vec
                  - text: in a
                  - link [ref=f84e1971]:
                    - /url: ../mem/struct.ManuallyDrop.html
                    - code: ManuallyDrop
                  - text: is the typical way to achieve this.
                - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e1973]
                - code: "// Allocate vector big enough for 4 elements. let size = 4; let mut x: Vec<i32> = Vec::with_capacity(size); let x_ptr = x.as_mut_ptr(); // Initialize elements via raw pointer writes, then set length. unsafe { for i in 0..size { *x_ptr.add(i) = i as i32; } x.set_len(size); } assert_eq!(&*x, &[0, 1, 2, 3]);"
                - paragraph: "Due to the aliasing guarantee, the following code is legal:"
                - code: "unsafe { let mut v = vec![0]; let ptr1 = v.as_mut_ptr(); ptr1.write(1); let ptr2 = v.as_mut_ptr(); ptr2.write(2); // Notably, the write to `ptr2` did *not* invalidate `ptr1`: ptr1.write(3); }"
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Deallocating a vector using
                  - link [ref=f84e1982]:
                    - code: Box
