                  - code: x
                  - text: in the vector (
                  - code: "[.., p, .., x, ..]"
                  - text: ), so
                  - code: same_bucket(x, p)
                  - text: is receiving them in reversed order (unlike
                  - link [ref=f84e2293]:
                    - /url: ../primitive.slice.html#method.windows
                    - code: windows
                  - text: ).
                - paragraph: If the vector is sorted, this removes all duplicates. For more complicated predicates however, the order (ascending vs. descending) can matter.
                - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e2296]
                - code: let mut vec = vec!["foo", "bar", "Bar", "baz", "bar"]; vec.dedup_by(|x, p| x.eq_ignore_ascii_case(p)); assert_eq!(vec, ["foo", "bar", "baz", "bar"]);
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Both references passed to
                  - code: same_bucket
                  - text: are mutable. This allows merging elements by mutating
                  - code: p
                  - text: and returning
                  - code: "true"
                  - text: ":"
                - code: "let mut ranges = vec![1..2, 2..4, 2..5, 8..9]; // Sort ranges by start, and if equal, by end (lexicographically) // Sorting in reverse instead (`x.start.cmp(&p.start)...`) would later fail ranges.sort_unstable_by(|p, x| p.start.cmp(&x.start).then(p.end.cmp(&x.end))); // Merge touching (`1..2` and `2..4`) and then overlapping (`1..4` and `2..5`) ranges ranges.dedup_by(|x, p| { if p.end >= x.start { p.end = p.end.max(x.end); true } else { false } }); assert_eq!(ranges, [1..5, 8..9]);"
            - group:
              - generic:
                - generic:
                  - link "Source" [ref=f84e2310]: ../../src/alloc/vec/mod.rs.html#2835
                  - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e2311]:
                    - text: pub fn
                    - link "push_within_capacity" [ref=f84e2312]
                    - text: "(&mut self, value: T) ->"
                    - link "Result" [ref=f84e2313]
                    - text: <
                    - link "&mut T" [ref=f84e2314]
                    - text: ", T>"
                - generic:
                  - text: � 🔬
                  - generic:
                    - text: This is a nightly-only experimental API. (
                    - code: vec_push_within_capacity
                    - link "#100486" [ref=f84e2319]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/100486
                    - text: )
                - text: Collapse
              - generic:
                - paragraph: Appends an element and returns a reference to it if there is sufficient spare capacity, otherwise an error is returned with the element.
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Unlike
                  - link [ref=f84e2323]:
                    - code: push
                  - text: this method will not reallocate when there’s insufficient capacity. The caller should use
                  - link [ref=f84e2325]:
                    - code: reserve
                  - text: or
                  - link [ref=f84e2327]:
                    - code: try_reserve
                  - text: to ensure that there is enough capacity.
                - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e2329]
                - paragraph:
                  - text: A manual, panic-free alternative to
                  - link [ref=f84e2331]:
                    - /url: ../iter/trait.FromIterator.html
                    - code: FromIterator
                  - text: ":"
                - code: "#![feature(vec_push_within_capacity)] use std::collections::TryReserveError; fn from_iter_fallible<T>(iter: impl Iterator<Item=T>) -> Result<Vec<T>, TryReserveError> { let mut vec = Vec::new(); for value in iter { if let Err(value) = vec.push_within_capacity(value) { vec.try_reserve(1)?; // this cannot fail, the previous line either returned or added at least 1 free slot let _ = vec.push_within_capacity(value); } } Ok(vec) } assert_eq!(from_iter_fallible(0..100), Ok(Vec::from_iter(0..100)));"
                - heading "Time complexity" [level=5] [ref=f84e2336]
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Takes
                  - emphasis: O
                  - text: (1) time.
            - group:
              - generic:
                - generic:
                  - generic:
                    - text: 1.0.0 ·
                    - link "Source" [ref=f84e2343]: ../../src/alloc/vec/mod.rs.html#2872
                  - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e2344]:
                    - text: pub fn
