                  - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e2428]:
                    - text: pub fn
                    - link "drain" [ref=f84e2429]
                    - text: "<R>(&mut self, range: R) ->"
                    - link "Drain" [ref=f84e2430]
                    - text: <'_, T, A>
                    - link "ⓘ" [ref=f84e2431]
                    - generic:
                      - text: "where R:"
                      - link "RangeBounds" [ref=f84e2433]: ../ops/trait.RangeBounds.html
                      - text: <
                      - link "usize" [ref=f84e2434]
                      - text: ">,"
                - text: Collapse
              - generic:
                - paragraph: Removes the subslice indicated by the given range from the vector, returning a double-ended iterator over the removed subslice.
                - paragraph: If the iterator is dropped before being fully consumed, it drops the remaining removed elements.
                - paragraph: The returned iterator keeps a mutable borrow on the vector to optimize its implementation.
                - heading "Panics" [level=5] [ref=f84e2439]
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Panics if the range has
                  - code: start_bound > end_bound
                  - text: ", or, if the range is bounded on either end and past the length of the vector."
                - heading "Leaking" [level=5] [ref=f84e2442]
                - paragraph:
                  - text: If the returned iterator goes out of scope without being dropped (due to
                  - link [ref=f84e2444]:
                    - /url: ../mem/fn.forget.html
                    - code: mem::forget
                  - text: ", for example), the vector may have lost and leaked elements arbitrarily, including elements outside the range."
                - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e2446]
                - code: "let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3]; let u: Vec<_> = v.drain(1..).collect(); assert_eq!(v, &[1]); assert_eq!(u, &[2, 3]); // A full range clears the vector, like `clear()` does v.drain(..); assert_eq!(v, &[]);"
            - group:
              - generic:
                - generic:
                  - generic:
                    - text: 1.0.0 ·
                    - link "Source" [ref=f84e2454]: ../../src/alloc/vec/mod.rs.html#3068
