                - text: Collapse
              - generic:
                - paragraph: Splits the collection into two at the given index.
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Returns a newly allocated vector containing the elements in the range
                  - code: "[at, len)"
                  - text: . After the call, the original vector will be left containing the elements
                  - code: "[0, at)"
                  - text: with its previous capacity unchanged.
                - list:
                  - listitem:
                    - text: If you want to take ownership of the entire contents and capacity of the vector, see
                    - link [ref=f84e2511]:
                      - /url: ../mem/fn.take.html
                      - code: mem::take
                    - text: or
                    - link [ref=f84e2513]:
                      - /url: ../mem/fn.replace.html
                      - code: mem::replace
                    - text: .
                  - listitem:
                    - text: If you don’t need the returned vector at all, see
                    - link [ref=f84e2516]:
                      - code: Vec::truncate
                    - text: .
                  - listitem:
                    - text: If you want to take ownership of an arbitrary subslice, or you don’t necessarily want to store the removed items in a vector, see
                    - link [ref=f84e2519]:
                      - code: Vec::drain
                    - text: .
                - heading "Panics" [level=5] [ref=f84e2521]
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Panics if
                  - code: at > len
                  - text: .
                - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e2524]
                - code: let mut vec = vec!['a', 'b', 'c']; let vec2 = vec.split_off(1); assert_eq!(vec, ['a']); assert_eq!(vec2, ['b', 'c']);
            - group:
              - generic:
                - generic:
                  - generic:
                    - text: 1.33.0 ·
                    - link "Source" [ref=f84e2532]: ../../src/alloc/vec/mod.rs.html#3219-3221
                  - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e2533]:
                    - text: pub fn
                    - link "resize_with" [ref=f84e2534]
                    - text: "<F>(&mut self, new_len:"
                    - link "usize" [ref=f84e2535]
                    - text: ", f: F)"
                    - generic:
                      - text: "where F:"
                      - link "FnMut" [ref=f84e2537]
                      - text: () -> T,
                - text: Collapse
              - generic:
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Resizes the
                  - code: Vec
                  - text: in-place so that
                  - code: len
                  - text: is equal to
                  - code: new_len
                  - text: .
                - paragraph:
                  - text: If
                  - code: new_len
                  - text: is greater than
                  - code: len
                  - text: ", the"
                  - code: Vec
                  - text: is extended by the difference, with each additional slot filled with the result of calling the closure
                  - code: f
                  - text: . The return values from
                  - code: f
                  - text: will end up in the
                  - code: Vec
                  - text: in the order they have been generated.
                - paragraph:
                  - text: If
                  - code: new_len
                  - text: is less than
                  - code: len
                  - text: ", the"
                  - code: Vec
                  - text: is simply truncated.
                - paragraph:
                  - text: This method uses a closure to create new values on every push. If you’d rather
                  - link [ref=f84e2555]:
                    - code: Clone
                  - text: a given value, use
                  - link [ref=f84e2557]:
                    - code: Vec::resize
                  - text: . If you want to use the
                  - link [ref=f84e2559]:
                    - /url: ../default/trait.Default.html
                    - code: Default
                  - text: trait to generate values, you can pass
                  - link [ref=f84e2561]:
                    - /url: ../default/trait.Default.html#tymethod.default
                    - code: Default::default
                  - text: as the second argument.
                - heading "Panics" [level=5] [ref=f84e2563]
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Panics if the new capacity exceeds
                  - code: isize::MAX
                  - emphasis: bytes
                  - text: .
                - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e2567]
                - code: "let mut vec = vec![1, 2, 3]; vec.resize_with(5, Default::default); assert_eq!(vec, [1, 2, 3, 0, 0]); let mut vec = vec![]; let mut p = 1; vec.resize_with(4, || { p *= 2; p }); assert_eq!(vec, [2, 4, 8, 16]);"
            - group:
              - generic:
                - generic:
                  - generic:
                    - text: 1.47.0 ·
                    - link "Source" [ref=f84e2575]: ../../src/alloc/vec/mod.rs.html#3261-3263
                  - 'heading "pub fn leak<''a>(self) -> &''a mut [T] where A: ''a," [level=4] [ref=f84e2576]':
                    - text: pub fn
                    - link "leak" [ref=f84e2577]
                    - text: <'a>(self) -> &'a mut
                    - link "[T]" [ref=f84e2578]
                    - generic: "where A: 'a,"
                - text: Collapse
              - generic:
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Consumes and leaks the
                  - code: Vec
                  - text: ", returning a mutable reference to the contents,"
                  - code: "&'a mut [T]"
                  - text: .
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Note that the type
                  - code: T
                  - text: must outlive the chosen lifetime
                  - code: "'a"
                  - text: . If the type has only static references, or none at all, then this may be chosen to be
                  - code: "'static"
                  - text: .
                - paragraph:
                  - text: As of Rust 1.57, this method does not reallocate or shrink the
                  - code: Vec
                  - text: ", so the leaked allocation may include unused capacity that is not part of the returned slice."
                - paragraph: This function is mainly useful for data that lives for the remainder of the program’s life. Dropping the returned reference will cause a memory leak.
                - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e2591]
                - paragraph: "Simple usage:"
                - code: "let x = vec![1, 2, 3]; let static_ref: &'static mut [usize] = x.leak(); static_ref[0] += 1; assert_eq!(static_ref, &[2, 2, 3]);"
            - group:
              - generic:
                - generic:
                  - generic:
                    - generic "Stable since Rust version 1.60.0, const unstable":
                      - text: "1.60.0 (const:"
                      - link "unstable" [ref=f84e2601]
                      - text: )
                    - link "Source" [ref=f84e2602]: ../../src/alloc/vec/mod.rs.html#3300
                  - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e2603]:
                    - text: pub fn
                    - link "spare_capacity_mut" [ref=f84e2604]
                    - text: (&mut self) -> &mut [
                    - link "MaybeUninit" [ref=f84e2605]
                    - text: <T>]
                - text: Collapse
              - generic:
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Returns the remaining spare capacity of the vector as a slice of
                  - code: MaybeUninit<T>
                  - text: .
                - paragraph:
                  - text: The returned slice can be used to fill the vector with data (e.g. by reading from a file) before marking the data as initialized using the
                  - link [ref=f84e2610]:
                    - /url: struct.Vec.html#method.set_len
                    - code: set_len
                  - text: method.
                - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e2612]
                - code: "// Allocate vector big enough for 10 elements. let mut v = Vec::with_capacity(10); // Fill in the first 3 elements. let uninit = v.spare_capacity_mut(); uninit[0].write(0); uninit[1].write(1); uninit[2].write(2); // Mark the first 3 elements of the vector as being initialized. unsafe { v.set_len(3); } assert_eq!(&v, &[0, 1, 2]);"
            - group:
              - generic:
                - generic:
                  - link "Source" [ref=f84e2619]: ../../src/alloc/vec/mod.rs.html#3366
                  - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e2620]:
                    - text: pub const fn
                    - link "split_at_spare_mut" [ref=f84e2621]
                    - text: (&mut self) -> (&mut
                    - link "[T]" [ref=f84e2622]
                    - text: ", &mut ["
                    - link "MaybeUninit" [ref=f84e2623]
                    - text: <T>])
                - generic:
                  - text: � 🔬
                  - generic:
                    - text: This is a nightly-only experimental API. (
                    - code: vec_split_at_spare
                    - link "#81944" [ref=f84e2628]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/81944
                    - text: )
                - text: Collapse
              - generic:
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Returns vector content as a slice of
                  - code: T
                  - text: ", along with the remaining spare capacity of the vector as a slice of"
                  - code: MaybeUninit<T>
                  - text: .
                - paragraph:
                  - text: The returned spare capacity slice can be used to fill the vector with data (e.g. by reading from a file) before marking the data as initialized using the
                  - link [ref=f84e2634]:
                    - code: set_len
                  - text: method.
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Note that this is a low-level API, which should be used with care for optimization purposes. If you need to append data to a
                  - code: Vec
                  - text: you can use
                  - link [ref=f84e2638]:
                    - code: push
                  - link [ref=f84e2640]:
                    - /url: struct.Vec.html#method.extend
                    - code: extend
                  - link [ref=f84e2642]:
                    - /url: struct.Vec.html#method.extend_from_slice
                    - code: extend_from_slice
                  - link [ref=f84e2644]:
                    - /url: struct.Vec.html#method.extend_from_within
                    - code: extend_from_within
                  - link [ref=f84e2646]:
                    - code: insert
                  - link [ref=f84e2648]:
                    - /url: struct.Vec.html#method.append
                    - code: append
                  - link [ref=f84e2650]:
                    - code: resize
                  - text: or
                  - link [ref=f84e2652]:
                    - /url: struct.Vec.html#method.resize_with
                    - code: resize_with
                  - text: ", depending on your exact needs."
                - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e2654]
                - code: "#![feature(vec_split_at_spare)] let mut v = vec![1, 1, 2]; // Reserve additional space big enough for 10 elements. v.reserve(10); let (init, uninit) = v.split_at_spare_mut(); let sum = init.iter().copied().sum::<u32>(); // Fill in the next 4 elements. uninit[0].write(sum); uninit[1].write(sum * 2); uninit[2].write(sum * 3); uninit[3].write(sum * 4); // Mark the 4 elements of the vector as being initialized. unsafe { let len = v.len(); v.set_len(len + 4); } assert_eq!(&v, &[1, 1, 2, 4, 8, 12, 16]);"
            - group:
              - generic:
                - generic:
                  - link "Source" [ref=f84e2661]: ../../src/alloc/vec/mod.rs.html#3425
                  - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e2662]:
