                  - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e2903]:
                    - text: pub fn
                    - link "splice" [ref=f84e2904]
                    - text: "<R, I>( &mut self, range: R, replace_with: I, ) ->"
                    - link "Splice" [ref=f84e2905]
                    - text: <'_, <I as
                    - link "IntoIterator" [ref=f84e2906]: ../iter/trait.IntoIterator.html
                    - text: ">::"
                    - link "IntoIter" [ref=f84e2907]: ../iter/trait.IntoIterator.html#associatedtype.IntoIter
                    - text: ", A>"
                    - link "ⓘ" [ref=f84e2908]
                    - generic:
                      - text: "where R:"
                      - link "RangeBounds" [ref=f84e2910]
                      - text: <
                      - link "usize" [ref=f84e2911]
                      - text: ">, I:"
                      - link "IntoIterator" [ref=f84e2912]
                      - text: <Item = T>,
                - text: Collapse
              - generic:
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Creates a splicing iterator that replaces the specified range in the vector with the given
                  - code: replace_with
                  - text: iterator and yields the removed items.
                  - code: replace_with
                  - text: does not need to be the same length as
                  - code: range
                  - text: .
                - paragraph:
                  - code: range
                  - text: is removed even if the
                  - code: Splice
                  - text: iterator is not consumed before it is dropped.
                - paragraph:
                  - text: It is unspecified how many elements are removed from the vector if the
                  - code: Splice
                  - text: value is leaked.
                - paragraph:
                  - text: The input iterator
                  - code: replace_with
                  - text: is only consumed when the
                  - code: Splice
                  - text: value is dropped.
                - paragraph: "This is optimal if:"
                - list:
                  - listitem:
                    - text: The tail (elements in the vector after
                    - code: range
                    - text: ) is empty,
                  - listitem:
                    - text: or
                    - code: replace_with
                    - text: yields fewer or equal elements than
                    - code: range
                    - text: ’s length
                  - listitem:
                    - text: or the lower bound of its
                    - code: size_hint()
                    - text: is exact.
                - paragraph: Otherwise, a temporary vector is allocated and the tail is moved twice.
                - heading "Panics" [level=5] [ref=f84e2936]
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Panics if the range has
                  - code: start_bound > end_bound
                  - text: ", or, if the range is bounded on either end and past the length of the vector."
                - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e2939]
                - code: "let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3, 4]; let new = [7, 8, 9]; let u: Vec<_> = v.splice(1..3, new).collect(); assert_eq!(v, [1, 7, 8, 9, 4]); assert_eq!(u, [2, 3]);"
                - paragraph:
                  - text: Using
                  - code: splice
                  - text: "to insert new items into a vector efficiently at a specific position indicated by an empty range:"
                - code: let mut v = vec![1, 5]; let new = [2, 3, 4]; v.splice(1..1, new); assert_eq!(v, [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]);
            - group:
              - generic:
                - generic:
                  - generic:
                    - text: 1.87.0 ·
                    - link "Source" [ref=f84e2952]: ../../src/alloc/vec/mod.rs.html#4279-4282
                  - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e2953]:
                    - text: pub fn
                    - link "extract_if" [ref=f84e2954]
                    - text: "<F, R>( &mut self, range: R, filter: F, ) ->"
                    - link "ExtractIf" [ref=f84e2955]
                    - text: <'_, T, F, A>
                    - link "ⓘ" [ref=f84e2956]
                    - generic:
                      - text: "where F:"
                      - link "FnMut" [ref=f84e2958]
                      - text: (
                      - link "&mut T" [ref=f84e2959]
                      - text: ) ->
                      - link "bool" [ref=f84e2960]
                      - text: ", R:"
                      - link "RangeBounds" [ref=f84e2961]
                      - text: <
                      - link "usize" [ref=f84e2962]
                      - text: ">,"
                - text: Collapse
              - generic:
                - paragraph: Creates an iterator which uses a closure to determine if an element in the range should be removed.
                - paragraph:
                  - text: If the closure returns
                  - code: "true"
                  - text: ", the element is removed from the vector and yielded. If the closure returns"
                  - code: "false"
                  - text: ", or panics, the element remains in the vector and will not be yielded."
                - paragraph: Only elements that fall in the provided range are considered for extraction, but any elements after the range will still have to be moved if any element has been extracted.
                - paragraph:
                  - text: If the returned
                  - code: ExtractIf
                  - text: is not exhausted, e.g. because it is dropped without iterating or the iteration short-circuits, then the remaining elements will be retained. Use
                  - code: extract_if().for_each(drop)
                  - text: if you do not need the returned iterator, or
                  - link [ref=f84e2972]:
                    - /url: struct.Vec.html#method.retain_mut
                    - code: retain_mut
                  - text: with a negated predicate if you also do not need to restrict the range.
                - paragraph: "Using this method is equivalent to the following code:"
                - code: "let mut i = range.start; let end_items = vec.len() - range.end; while i < vec.len() - end_items { if some_predicate(&mut vec[i]) { let val = vec.remove(i); // your code here } else { i += 1; } }"
                - paragraph:
                  - text: But
                  - code: extract_if
                  - text: is easier to use.
                  - code: extract_if
                  - text: is also more efficient, because it can backshift the elements of the array in bulk.
                - paragraph: The iterator also lets you mutate the value of each element in the closure, regardless of whether you choose to keep or remove it.
                - heading "Panics" [level=5] [ref=f84e2982]
                - paragraph:
                  - text: If
                  - code: range
                  - text: is out of bounds.
                - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e2985]
                - paragraph: "Splitting a vector into even and odd values, reusing the original vector:"
                - code: let mut numbers = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 11, 13, 14, 15]; let evens = numbers.extract_if(.., |x| *x % 2 == 0).collect::<Vec<_>>(); let odds = numbers; assert_eq!(evens, vec![2, 4, 6, 8, 14]); assert_eq!(odds, vec![1, 3, 5, 9, 11, 13, 15]);
                - paragraph: "Using the range argument to only process a part of the vector:"
                - code: let mut items = vec![0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2]; let ones = items.extract_if(7.., |x| *x == 1).collect::<Vec<_>>(); assert_eq!(items, vec![0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 2, 2, 2]); assert_eq!(ones.len(), 3);
