                  - link "rchunks_exact_mut" [ref=f84e4189]
                  - text: "(&mut self, chunk_size:"
                  - link "usize" [ref=f84e4190]
                  - text: ) ->
                  - link "RChunksExactMut" [ref=f84e4191]: ../slice/struct.RChunksExactMut.html
                  - text: <'_, T>
                  - link "ⓘ" [ref=f84e4192]
              - text: Collapse
            - generic:
              - paragraph:
                - text: Returns an iterator over
                - code: chunk_size
                - text: elements of the slice at a time, starting at the end of the slice.
              - paragraph:
                - text: The chunks are mutable slices, and do not overlap. If
                - code: chunk_size
                - text: does not divide the length of the slice, then the last up to
                - code: chunk_size-1
                - text: elements will be omitted and can be retrieved from the
                - code: into_remainder
                - text: function of the iterator.
              - paragraph:
                - text: Due to each chunk having exactly
                - code: chunk_size
                - text: elements, the compiler can often optimize the resulting code better than in the case of
                - link [ref=f84e4202]:
                  - code: chunks_mut
                - text: .
              - paragraph:
                - text: See
                - link [ref=f84e4205]:
                  - code: rchunks_mut
                - text: for a variant of this iterator that also returns the remainder as a smaller chunk, and
                - link [ref=f84e4207]:
                  - code: chunks_exact_mut
                - text: for the same iterator but starting at the beginning of the slice.
              - paragraph:
                - text: If your
                - code: chunk_size
                - text: is a constant, consider using
                - link [ref=f84e4211]:
                  - code: as_rchunks_mut
                - text: instead, which will give references to arrays of exactly that length, rather than slices.
              - heading "Panics" [level=5] [ref=f84e4213]
              - paragraph:
                - text: Panics if
                - code: chunk_size
                - text: is zero.
              - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e4216]
              - code: "let v = &mut [0, 0, 0, 0, 0]; let mut count = 1; for chunk in v.rchunks_exact_mut(2) { for elem in chunk.iter_mut() { *elem += count; } count += 1; } assert_eq!(v, &[0, 2, 2, 1, 1]);"
          - group:
            - generic:
              - generic:
                - generic:
                  - text: 1.77.0 ·
                  - link "Source" [ref=f84e4224]: ../../src/core/slice/mod.rs.html#1873-1875
                - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e4225]:
                  - text: pub fn
                  - link "chunk_by" [ref=f84e4226]
                  - text: "<F>(&self, pred: F) ->"
                  - link "ChunkBy" [ref=f84e4227]: ../slice/struct.ChunkBy.html
                  - text: <'_, T, F>
                  - link "ⓘ" [ref=f84e4228]
                  - generic:
                    - text: "where F:"
                    - link "FnMut" [ref=f84e4230]
                    - text: (
                    - link "&T" [ref=f84e4231]
                    - link "&T" [ref=f84e4232]
                    - text: ) ->
                    - link "bool" [ref=f84e4233]
              - text: Collapse
            - generic:
              - paragraph: Returns an iterator over the slice producing non-overlapping runs of elements using the predicate to separate them.
              - paragraph:
                - text: The predicate is called for every pair of consecutive elements, meaning that it is called on
                - code: slice[0]
                - text: and
                - code: slice[1]
                - text: ", followed by"
                - code: slice[1]
                - text: and
                - code: slice[2]
                - text: ", and so on."
              - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e4241]
              - code: let slice = &[1, 1, 1, 3, 3, 2, 2, 2]; let mut iter = slice.chunk_by(|a, b| a == b); assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&[1, 1, 1][..])); assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&[3, 3][..])); assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&[2, 2, 2][..])); assert_eq!(iter.next(), None);
              - paragraph: "This method can be used to extract the sorted subslices:"
              - code: let slice = &[1, 1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4]; let mut iter = slice.chunk_by(|a, b| a <= b); assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&[1, 1, 2, 3][..])); assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&[2, 3][..])); assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&[2, 3, 4][..])); assert_eq!(iter.next(), None);
