                  - text: )
              - text: Collapse
            - generic:
              - paragraph:
                - text: Partially sorts the slice in ascending order
                - strong: without
                - text: preserving the initial order of equal elements.
              - paragraph:
                - text: Upon completion, for the specified range
                - code: start..end
                - text: ", it’s guaranteed that:"
              - list:
                - listitem:
                  - text: Every element in
                  - code: self[..start]
                  - text: is smaller than or equal to
                - listitem:
                  - text: Every element in
                  - code: self[start..end]
                  - text: ", which is sorted, and smaller than or equal to"
                - listitem:
                  - text: Every element in
                  - code: self[end..]
                  - text: .
              - paragraph: This partial sort is unstable, meaning it may reorder equal elements in the specified range. It may reorder elements outside the specified range as well, but the guarantees above still hold.
              - paragraph:
                - text: This partial sort is in-place (i.e., does not allocate), and
                - emphasis: O
                - text: (
                - emphasis: "n"
                - text: +
                - emphasis: k
                - text: "* log("
                - emphasis: k
                - text: )) worst-case, where
                - emphasis: "n"
                - text: is the length of the slice and
                - emphasis: k
                - text: is the length of the specified range.
              - paragraph:
                - text: See the documentation of
                - link [ref=f84e5280]:
                  - /url: ../primitive.slice.html#method.sort_unstable
                  - code: sort_unstable
                - text: for implementation notes.
              - heading "Panics" [level=5] [ref=f84e5282]
              - paragraph:
                - text: May panic if the implementation of
                - link [ref=f84e5284]:
                  - code: Ord
                - text: for
                - code: T
                - text: does not implement a total order, or if the
                - link [ref=f84e5287]:
                  - code: Ord
                - text: implementation panics, or if the specified range is out of bounds.
              - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e5289]
              - code: "#![feature(slice_partial_sort_unstable)] let mut v = [4, -5, 1, -3, 2]; // empty range at the beginning, nothing changed v.partial_sort_unstable(0..0); assert_eq!(v, [4, -5, 1, -3, 2]); // empty range in the middle, partitioning the slice v.partial_sort_unstable(2..2); for i in 0..2 { assert!(v[i] <= v[2]); } for i in 3..v.len() { assert!(v[2] <= v[i]); } // single element range, same as select_nth_unstable v.partial_sort_unstable(2..3); for i in 0..2 { assert!(v[i] <= v[2]); } for i in 3..v.len() { assert!(v[2] <= v[i]); } // partial sort a subrange v.partial_sort_unstable(1..4); assert_eq!(&v[1..4], [-3, 1, 2]); // partial sort the whole range, same as sort_unstable v.partial_sort_unstable(..); assert_eq!(v, [-5, -3, 1, 2, 4]);"
