                  - link "Source" [ref=f84e5397]: ../../src/core/slice/mod.rs.html#3531-3533
                - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e5398]:
                  - text: pub fn
                  - link "select_nth_unstable" [ref=f84e5399]
                  - text: "( &mut self, index:"
                  - link "usize" [ref=f84e5400]
                  - text: ", ) -> (&mut"
                  - link "[T]" [ref=f84e5401]
                  - link "&mut T" [ref=f84e5402]
                  - text: ", &mut"
                  - link "[T]" [ref=f84e5403]
                  - text: )
                  - generic:
                    - text: "where T:"
                    - link "Ord" [ref=f84e5405]
              - text: Collapse
            - generic:
              - paragraph:
                - text: Reorders the slice such that the element at
                - code: index
                - text: is at a sort-order position. All elements before
                - code: index
                - text: will be
                - code: <=
                - text: to this value, and all elements after will be
                - code: ">="
                - text: to it.
              - paragraph:
                - text: This reordering is unstable (i.e. any element that compares equal to the nth element may end up at that position), in-place (i.e. does not allocate), and runs in
                - emphasis: O
                - text: (
                - emphasis: "n"
                - text: ) time. This function is also known as “kth element” in other libraries.
              - paragraph: "Returns a triple that partitions the reordered slice:"
              - list:
                - listitem:
                  - paragraph:
                    - text: The unsorted subslice before
                    - code: index
                    - text: ", whose elements all satisfy"
                    - code: x <= self[index]
                    - text: .
                - listitem:
                  - paragraph:
                    - text: The element at
                    - code: index
                    - text: .
                - listitem:
                  - paragraph:
                    - text: The unsorted subslice after
                    - code: index
                    - text: ", whose elements all satisfy"
                    - code: x >= self[index]
                    - text: .
              - heading "Current implementation" [level=5] [ref=f84e5428]
              - paragraph:
                - text: The current algorithm is an introselect implementation based on
                - link "ipnsort" [ref=f84e5430]
                - text: by Lukas Bergdoll and Orson Peters, which is also the basis for
                - link [ref=f84e5431]:
                  - code: sort_unstable
                - text: . The fallback algorithm is Median of Medians using Tukey’s Ninther for pivot selection, which guarantees linear runtime for all inputs.
              - heading "Panics" [level=5] [ref=f84e5433]
              - paragraph:
                - text: Panics when
                - code: index >= len()
                - text: ", and so always panics on empty slices."
              - paragraph:
                - text: May panic if the implementation of
                - link [ref=f84e5437]:
                  - code: Ord
                - text: for
                - code: T
                - text: does not implement a
                - link "total order" [ref=f84e5440]
                - text: .
              - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e5441]
              - code: "let mut v = [-5i32, 4, 2, -3, 1]; // Find the items `<=` to the median, the median itself, and the items `>=` to it. let (lesser, median, greater) = v.select_nth_unstable(2); assert!(lesser == [-3, -5] || lesser == [-5, -3]); assert_eq!(median, &mut 1); assert!(greater == [4, 2] || greater == [2, 4]); // We are only guaranteed the slice will be one of the following, based on the way we sort // about the specified index. assert!(v == [-3, -5, 1, 2, 4] || v == [-5, -3, 1, 2, 4] || v == [-3, -5, 1, 4, 2] || v == [-5, -3, 1, 4, 2]);"
          - group:
