              - text: Collapse
            - generic:
              - paragraph: Returns mutable references to many indices at once, without doing any checks.
              - paragraph:
                - text: An index can be either a
                - code: usize
                - text: ", a"
                - link [ref=f84e6342]:
                  - code: Range
                - text: or a
                - link [ref=f84e6344]:
                  - /url: ../ops/struct.RangeInclusive.html
                  - code: RangeInclusive
                - text: . Note that this method takes an array, so all indices must be of the same type. If passed an array of
                - code: usize
                - text: s this method gives back an array of mutable references to single elements, while if passed an array of ranges it gives back an array of mutable references to slices.
              - paragraph:
                - text: For a safe alternative see
                - link [ref=f84e6348]:
                  - /url: ../primitive.slice.html#method.get_disjoint_mut
                  - code: get_disjoint_mut
                - text: .
              - heading "Safety" [level=5] [ref=f84e6350]
              - paragraph:
                - text: Calling this method with overlapping or out-of-bounds indices is
                - emphasis:
                  - link "undefined behavior" [ref=f84e6353]
                - text: even if the resulting references are not used.
              - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e6354]
              - code: "let x = &mut [1, 2, 4]; unsafe { let [a, b] = x.get_disjoint_unchecked_mut([0, 2]); *a *= 10; *b *= 100; } assert_eq!(x, &[10, 2, 400]); unsafe { let [a, b] = x.get_disjoint_unchecked_mut([0..1, 1..3]); a[0] = 8; b[0] = 88; b[1] = 888; } assert_eq!(x, &[8, 88, 888]); unsafe { let [a, b] = x.get_disjoint_unchecked_mut([1..=2, 0..=0]); a[0] = 11; a[1] = 111; b[0] = 1; } assert_eq!(x, &[1, 11, 111]);"
          - group:
