            - generic:
              - paragraph: Returns the range of indices that a subslice points to.
              - paragraph:
                - text: Returns
                - code: None
                - text: if
                - code: subslice
                - text: does not point within the slice or if it is not aligned with the elements in the slice.
              - paragraph:
                - text: This method
                - strong: does not compare elements
                - text: . Instead, this method finds the location in the slice that
                - code: subslice
                - text: was obtained from. To find the index of a subslice via comparison, instead use
                - link [ref=f84e6446]:
                  - code: .windows()
                - link [ref=f84e6448]:
                  - code: .position()
                - text: .
              - paragraph:
                - text: This method is useful for extending slice iterators like
                - link [ref=f84e6451]:
                  - code: slice::split
                - text: .
              - paragraph:
                - text: Note that this may return a false positive (either
                - code: Some(0..0)
                - text: or
                - code: Some(self.len()..self.len())
                - text: ) if
                - code: subslice
                - text: has a length of zero and points to the beginning or end of another, separate, slice.
              - heading "Panics" [level=5] [ref=f84e6457]
              - paragraph:
                - text: Panics if
                - code: T
                - text: is zero-sized.
              - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e6460]
              - paragraph: "Basic usage:"
              - code: "use core::range::Range; let nums = &[0, 5, 10, 0, 0, 5]; let mut iter = nums .split(|t| *t == 0) .map(|n| nums.subslice_range(n).unwrap()); assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(Range { start: 0, end: 0 })); assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(Range { start: 1, end: 3 })); assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(Range { start: 4, end: 4 })); assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(Range { start: 5, end: 6 }));"
          - group:
            - generic:
              - generic:
                - link "Source" [ref=f84e6468]: ../../src/core/slice/mod.rs.html#5367
