          - group:
            - generic:
              - generic:
                - generic:
                  - text: 1.0.0 ·
                  - link "Source" [ref=f84e6507]: ../../src/alloc/slice.rs.html#131-133
                - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e6508]:
                  - text: pub fn
                  - link "sort" [ref=f84e6509]
                  - text: (&mut self)
                  - generic:
                    - text: "where T:"
                    - link "Ord" [ref=f84e6511]
              - text: Collapse
            - generic:
              - paragraph: Sorts the slice in ascending order, preserving initial order of equal elements.
              - paragraph:
                - text: This sort is stable (i.e., does not reorder equal elements) and
                - emphasis: O
                - text: (
                - emphasis: "n"
                - text: "* log("
                - emphasis: "n"
                - text: )) worst-case.
              - paragraph:
                - text: If the implementation of
                - link [ref=f84e6519]:
                  - code: Ord
                - text: for
                - code: T
                - text: does not implement a
                - link "total order" [ref=f84e6522]
                - text: ", the function may panic; even if the function exits normally, the resulting order of elements in the slice is unspecified. See also the note on panicking below."
              - paragraph:
                - text: When applicable, unstable sorting is preferred because it is generally faster than stable sorting and it doesn’t allocate auxiliary memory. See
                - link [ref=f84e6524]:
                  - code: sort_unstable
                - text: . The exception are partially sorted slices, which may be better served with
                - code: slice::sort
                - text: .
              - paragraph:
                - text: Sorting types that only implement
                - link [ref=f84e6528]:
                  - code: PartialOrd
                - text: such as
                - link [ref=f84e6530]:
                  - code: f32
                - text: and
                - link [ref=f84e6532]:
                  - code: f64
                - text: require additional precautions. For example,
                - code: f32::NAN != f32::NAN
                - text: ", which doesn’t fulfill the reflexivity requirement of"
                - link [ref=f84e6535]:
                  - code: Ord
                - text: . By using an alternative comparison function with
                - code: slice::sort_by
                - text: such as
                - link [ref=f84e6538]:
                  - code: f32::total_cmp
                - text: or
                - link [ref=f84e6540]:
                  - code: f64::total_cmp
                - text: that defines a
                - link "total order" [ref=f84e6542]
                - text: users can sort slices containing floating-point values. Alternatively, if all values in the slice are guaranteed to be in a subset for which
                - link [ref=f84e6543]:
                  - code: PartialOrd::partial_cmp
                - text: forms a
                - link "total order" [ref=f84e6545]
                - text: ", it’s possible to sort the slice with"
                - code: sort_by(|a, b| a.partial_cmp(b).unwrap())
                - text: .
              - heading "Current implementation" [level=5] [ref=f84e6547]
              - paragraph:
                - text: The current implementation is based on
                - link "driftsort" [ref=f84e6549]: https://github.com/Voultapher/driftsort
                - text: by Orson Peters and Lukas Bergdoll, which combines the fast average case of quicksort with the fast worst case and partial run detection of mergesort, achieving linear time on fully sorted and reversed inputs. On inputs with k distinct elements, the expected time to sort the data is
                - emphasis: O
                - text: (
                - emphasis: "n"
                - text: "* log("
                - emphasis: k
                - text: )).
              - paragraph:
                - text: The auxiliary memory allocation behavior depends on the input length. Short slices are handled without allocation, medium sized slices allocate
                - code: self.len()
                - text: and beyond that it clamps at
                - code: self.len() / 2
                - text: .
              - heading "Panics" [level=5] [ref=f84e6556]
              - paragraph:
                - text: May panic if the implementation of
                - link [ref=f84e6558]:
                  - code: Ord
                - text: for
                - code: T
                - text: does not implement a
                - link "total order" [ref=f84e6561]
                - text: ", or if the"
                - link [ref=f84e6562]:
                  - code: Ord
                - text: implementation itself panics.
              - paragraph:
                - text: All safe functions on slices preserve the invariant that even if the function panics, all original elements will remain in the slice and any possible modifications via interior mutability are observed in the input. This ensures that recovery code (for instance inside of a
                - code: Drop
                - text: or following a
                - code: catch_unwind
                - text: ) will still have access to all the original elements. For instance, if the slice belongs to a
                - code: Vec
                - text: ", the"
                - code: Vec::drop
                - text: method will be able to dispose of all contained elements.
              - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e6569]
              - code: let mut v = [4, -5, 1, -3, 2]; v.sort(); assert_eq!(v, [-5, -3, 1, 2, 4]);
          - group:
            - generic:
              - generic:
                - generic:
                  - text: 1.0.0 ·
                  - link "Source" [ref=f84e6577]: ../../src/alloc/slice.rs.html#192-194
                - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e6578]:
                  - text: pub fn
                  - link "sort_by" [ref=f84e6579]
                  - text: "<F>(&mut self, compare: F)"
                  - generic:
                    - text: "where F:"
                    - link "FnMut" [ref=f84e6581]
                    - text: (
                    - link "&T" [ref=f84e6582]
                    - link "&T" [ref=f84e6583]
                    - text: ) ->
                    - link "Ordering" [ref=f84e6584]
              - text: Collapse
            - generic:
              - paragraph: Sorts the slice in ascending order with a comparison function, preserving initial order of equal elements.
              - paragraph:
                - text: This sort is stable (i.e., does not reorder equal elements) and
                - emphasis: O
                - text: (
                - emphasis: "n"
                - text: "* log("
                - emphasis: "n"
                - text: )) worst-case.
              - paragraph:
                - text: If the comparison function
                - code: compare
                - text: does not implement a
                - link "total order" [ref=f84e6593]
                - text: ", the function may panic; even if the function exits normally, the resulting order of elements in the slice is unspecified. See also the note on panicking below."
              - paragraph:
                - text: For example
                - code: "|a, b| (a - b).cmp(a)"
                - text: is a comparison function that is neither transitive nor reflexive nor total,
                - code: a < b < c < a
                - text: with
                - code: a = 1, b = 2, c = 3
                - text: . For more information and examples see the
                - link [ref=f84e6598]:
                  - code: Ord
                - text: documentation.
              - heading "Current implementation" [level=5] [ref=f84e6600]
              - paragraph:
                - text: The current implementation is based on
                - link "driftsort" [ref=f84e6602]
                - text: by Orson Peters and Lukas Bergdoll, which combines the fast average case of quicksort with the fast worst case and partial run detection of mergesort, achieving linear time on fully sorted and reversed inputs. On inputs with k distinct elements, the expected time to sort the data is
                - emphasis: O
                - text: (
                - emphasis: "n"
                - text: "* log("
                - emphasis: k
                - text: )).
              - paragraph:
                - text: The auxiliary memory allocation behavior depends on the input length. Short slices are handled without allocation, medium sized slices allocate
                - code: self.len()
                - text: and beyond that it clamps at
                - code: self.len() / 2
                - text: .
              - heading "Panics" [level=5] [ref=f84e6609]
              - paragraph:
                - text: May panic if
                - code: compare
                - text: does not implement a
                - link "total order" [ref=f84e6612]
                - text: ", or if"
                - code: compare
                - text: itself panics.
              - paragraph:
                - text: All safe functions on slices preserve the invariant that even if the function panics, all original elements will remain in the slice and any possible modifications via interior mutability are observed in the input. This ensures that recovery code (for instance inside of a
                - code: Drop
                - text: or following a
                - code: catch_unwind
                - text: ) will still have access to all the original elements. For instance, if the slice belongs to a
                - code: Vec
                - text: ", the"
                - code: Vec::drop
                - text: method will be able to dispose of all contained elements.
              - heading "Examples" [level=5] [ref=f84e6619]
              - code: let mut v = [4, -5, 1, -3, 2]; v.sort_by(|a, b| a.cmp(b)); assert_eq!(v, [-5, -3, 1, 2, 4]); // reverse sorting v.sort_by(|a, b| b.cmp(a)); assert_eq!(v, [4, 2, 1, -3, -5]);
          - group:
