                - text: 1.0.0 ·
                - link "Source" [ref=f84e7919]: ../../src/alloc/vec/mod.rs.html#3993
              - heading [level=3] [ref=f84e7920]:
                - text: impl<T>
                - link "FromIterator" [ref=f84e7921]
                - text: <T> for
                - link "Vec" [ref=f84e7922]
                - text: <T>
              - paragraph:
                - text: Collects an iterator into a Vec, commonly called via
                - link [ref=f84e7925]:
                  - /url: ../iter/trait.Iterator.html#method.collect
                  - code: Iterator::collect()
            - text: Collapse
          - generic:
            - heading "Allocation behavior" [level=4] [ref=f84e7928]
            - paragraph:
              - text: In general
              - code: Vec
              - text: does not guarantee any particular growth or allocation strategy. That also applies to this trait impl.
            - paragraph:
              - strong: "Note:"
              - text: This section covers implementation details and is therefore exempt from stability guarantees.
            - paragraph: "Vec may use any or none of the following strategies, depending on the supplied iterator:"
            - list:
              - listitem:
                - text: preallocate based on
                - link [ref=f84e7936]:
                  - /url: ../iter/trait.Iterator.html#method.size_hint
                  - code: Iterator::size_hint()
                - list:
                  - listitem: and panic if the number of items is outside the provided lower/upper bounds
              - listitem:
                - text: use an amortized growth strategy similar to
                - code: pushing
                - text: one item at a time
              - listitem: perform the iteration in-place on the original allocation backing the iterator
            - paragraph:
              - text: The last case warrants some attention. It is an optimization that in many cases reduces peak memory consumption and improves cache locality. But when big, short-lived allocations are created, only a small fraction of their items get collected, no further use is made of the spare capacity and the resulting
              - code: Vec
              - text: is moved into a longer-lived structure, then this can lead to the large allocations having their lifetimes unnecessarily extended which can result in increased memory footprint.
            - paragraph:
              - text: In cases where this is an issue, the excess capacity can be discarded with
              - link [ref=f84e7946]:
                - code: Vec::shrink_to()
              - link [ref=f84e7948]:
                - code: Vec::shrink_to_fit()
              - text: or by collecting into
              - link [ref=f84e7950]:
                - code: Box<[T]>
              - text: instead, which additionally reduces the size of the long-lived struct.
            - code: "static LONG_LIVED: Mutex<Vec<Vec<u16>>> = Mutex::new(Vec::new()); for i in 0..10 { let big_temporary: Vec<u16> = (0..1024).collect(); // discard most items let mut result: Vec<_> = big_temporary.into_iter().filter(|i| i % 100 == 0).collect(); // without this a lot of unused capacity might be moved into the global result.shrink_to_fit(); LONG_LIVED.lock().unwrap().push(result); }"
          - group:
            - generic:
              - generic:
                - link "Source" [ref=f84e7959]: ../../src/alloc/vec/mod.rs.html#3995
                - heading [level=4] [ref=f84e7960]:
                  - text: fn
                  - link "from_iter" [ref=f84e7961]: ../iter/trait.FromIterator.html#tymethod.from_iter
                  - text: "<I>(iter: I) ->"
                  - link "Vec" [ref=f84e7962]
                  - text: <T>
                  - generic:
                    - text: "where I:"
                    - link "IntoIterator" [ref=f84e7964]
                    - text: <Item = T>,
              - text: Collapse
            - generic:
              - text: Creates a value from an iterator.
              - link "Read more" [ref=f84e7966]
